<div class="focus-head friendly-focus">
<div><div class="kicker">Ways to strengthen legal security</div><h3>Durable protection is likely to come from several compatible layers, not one “higher” declaration</h3></div>
<p>There is no single mandatory sequence. Each route must be justified by competence, standing, remedy, territorial reach, implementation capacity and the concrete protection it adds.</p>
</div>
<div class="pathway-cards">
<article class="pathway-card"><div class="pathway-icon">LOCAL</div><h3>Municipal / provincial</h3><p><b>Could add:</b> Local normative coverage, implementation duties, budget/program integration, coordination and territorial legitimacy.</p><p class="control"><b>Current control:</b> Canchis is a verified foothold with real legal rank inside municipal competence. Its council can later modify or repeal it by ordinance, so implementation and institutional embedding matter. Additional provinces are options to assess, not mandatory “ratifications”.</p></article>
<article class="pathway-card"><div class="pathway-icon">REGION</div><h3>Regional</h3><p><b>Could add:</b> Cross-provincial coordination, regional policy and functions that genuinely belong to the regional level.</p><p class="control"><b>Current control:</b> A regional ordinance does not automatically override municipal law. Titicaca is the cautionary comparator: Puno's 2025 rights ordinance is listed as vigente, while national ministries publicly dispute its competence and legal efficacy. That is a validity/competence conflict, not a missing central-government “ratification”.</p></article>
<article class="pathway-card"><div class="pathway-icon">PUBLIC</div><h3>National / administrative</h3><p><b>Could add:</b> Sectoral enforcement, basin/water governance, environment, sanitation, public investment and — if politically viable — national legislation that addresses recognition or representation at a broader scale.</p><p class="control"><b>Current control:</b> Must be mapped authority-by-authority; a rights declaration does not replace sector competence. Titicaca also shows that national environmental-recovery law can coexist with a separate and contested rights-of-nature route.</p></article>
<article class="pathway-card"><div class="pathway-icon">COURT</div><h3>Judicial</h3><p><b>Could add:</b> Case-specific rights protection, enforceable orders and potentially a representation structure.</p><p class="control"><b>Current control:</b> Marañón is a real comparator. Standing, facts, defendants, evidence and remedy must be independently established for Willkamayu.</p></article>
<article class="pathway-card pathway-combined"><div class="pathway-icon">SYSTEM</div><h3>Combined protection</h3><p><b>Could add:</b> Evidence + implementation + legal accountability across the river system.</p><p class="control"><b>Current control:</b> Current strategic hypothesis; sequencing remains under research.</p></article>
</div>
<div class="focus-head friendly-focus">
<div><div class="kicker">Build with what already exists</div><h3>Different actors already hold important pieces of the river story</h3></div>
<p>Converging Waters should connect functions where useful, not absorb or rebrand work that already has owners and history.</p>
</div>
<div class="legal-grid actor-contribution-grid">
<article class="legal-card"><h3>CEPROSI + Terre des Hommes</h3><p>Long-term education, ceremony, cleanup, youth guardianship and process memory behind the Canchis milestone.</p></article>
<article class="legal-card"><h3>Riberas / ACIRIWI + Calca</h3><p>Riverbank recovery, environmental education, Corredor Verde and a verified municipal cooperation pathway in the middle corridor.</p></article>
<article class="legal-card"><h3>Women-led networks + public authorities</h3><p>Regional advocacy and articulation around contamination, headwaters and the Canchis precedent; no support for CW is inferred.</p></article>
</div>
<div class="legal-boundary working-direction">
<b>Current working direction:</b>
    preserve and operationalize the Canchis recognition, reduce its practical vulnerability through implementation and evidence, map the competent institutions along the full river, and add only those municipal, regional, national or judicial layers that produce real territorial reach, continuity or enforceability. The goal is not simply “more declarations”; it is a legally coherent protection architecture that is harder to reverse, ignore or fragment. The complete Canchis operative text, implementation acts and any formal Willkamayu representative remain open research dependencies.
  </div>
